            <ArticleTitle>Smoking, apolipoprotein E genotype, and early onset of coronary heart disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>268-70</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">We examined the association between smoking, apolipoprotein E (ApoE) genotype, and early onset of coronary heart disease (CHD).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Smoking behaviour and ApoE genotype of 904 patients with CHD aged 30-70 years were assessed with respect to age at onset of CHD.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The adjusted odds ratio for early onset of CHD (&lt; or =55 years) was 1.86 (95% confidence interval, 1.31-2.62) for smokers compared to non-smokers. The ApoE genotype had no significant influence and there was no significant interaction between ApoE genotype and smoking.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We found smoking but not ApoE genotype to be an independent risk factor for early onset of CHD.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
